\details{
Individual \eqn{i}, observed in year \eqn{t_i}, exerts on each candidate
destination \eqn{j} the hazard
\deqn{\lambda_{ij} = \exp\left(-\frac{d(s_i, v_j)^2}{2\sigma_i^2}\right)\,\theta_{j t_i}}
and the destination at which it is observed is a categorical draw with
probabilities \eqn{\pi_{ij} = \lambda_{ij} / \sum_k \lambda_{ik}}, the sum
running over the destinations operational in year \eqn{t_i}. The
log-likelihood is \eqn{\sum_i \log \pi_{i, e_i}} where \eqn{e_i} is the
observed destination.

Covariates enter through two linear predictors:
\eqn{\log \sigma_i = X_i \beta_\sigma} for individual-level covariates
(with intercept) and
\eqn{\log \theta_{jt} = Z_{jt} \beta_\theta + \log(\mathrm{effort}_{jt})}
for destination-level covariates, which may vary by year. Because the
normalisation is within individual and each individual belongs to one
year, a term constant across destinations within a year (an intercept, a
year effect) cancels and is refused.

With \code{cost} and \code{landscape} supplied, Euclidean distance is replaced by the
least-cost (ecological) distance through a resistance surface whose cell
cost is \eqn{\exp(\alpha^\top c(x))}, and \eqn{\alpha} is estimated
alongside the other parameters (coefficients named \verb{cost:}). The raster is
used at the resolution supplied and must cover all origins and
destinations with no missing cells; see \code{\link[=make_cost_fun]{make_cost_fun()}}.
\eqn{\alpha = 0} recovers (grid-approximate) Euclidean distance. Because
lattice paths are slightly longer than straight lines, the like-for-like
null for a resistance model is not the Euclidean fit but the same grid
with a flat surface: \code{cost = ~ 1} fixes every cell cost at 1, estimates no
resistance parameters, and the likelihood-ratio test between the two then
isolates the landscape effect.
By default the cost covariates are standardised over the raster cells
(\code{standardise_cost = TRUE}) so that \eqn{\exp(\alpha^\top c)} stays well
behaved during optimisation; \verb{cost:} coefficients are then per SD of the
covariate, and the means and SDs used are in \code{cost_fun$scale}.

Parameters are estimated on the log scale with \code{\link[stats:optim]{stats::optim()}} (BFGS);
standard errors are from the inverse numerical Hessian.
